\v 35 Then¦357375 the¦357378 iron¦357378, the¦357379 clay¦357379, the¦357380 bronze¦357380, the¦357381 silver¦357381, and¦357382 the¦357382 gold¦357382 were crushed¦357376 all at the same time and¦357383 became like¦357384 the \add ≈small pieces of¦357387 dry straw¦357384 at harvest time in\add* the summer¦357389. Then¦357390 the¦357392 wind¦357392 carried¦357390 them away¦357390 so that no trace of them¦357391 could be¦357398 found¦357398, \add ≈but¦357400\add* the¦357400 stone¦357400 that¦357402 struck¦357404 the¦357405 statue¦357405 became¦357406 a¦357407 huge¦357408 mountain¦357407 that then¦357409 filled¦357409 the¦357412 whole world¦357412.
\p
\v 36 “That¦357414 was the¦357415 dream¦357415, and¦357416 \add now\add* we'll¦357417 tell¦357417 the¦357420 king¦357420 its meaning:
\v 37 You¦357422, \add ≈your¦357425 majesty¦357425\add*, are¦357426 the¦357426 king¦357426 of¦357426 kings¦357427 who¦357428 has¦357435 been given¦357435 the¦357431 kingdom¦357431, the¦357432 power¦357432, the¦357433 strength¦357433, and¦357434 the splendour by the¦357429 god¦357429 of¦357429 the¦357430 heavens¦357430.
